text:
The great old sea-dog he was,
With all his crew around him;

There were some who had served 'im well
As long as three score years;

" The heart of woman, and her mind so bright, And tender as a lily's snow-white breast;
When she speaks out, what fire there is in it!
And how we all stand trembling to hear its sweet unrest."

Kashmiri:

Ye hai meri zindagi ke pahad-e-shor,
Ye hai Meri zindagani ki jigar se ghali;
Ek roz khade hu main, toh phook jaati hai,
Jis din ghum aur duaein humne sun li;


English:

These are my mountains of life's thunder,
these are my precious drops from the heart and soul;
When I stand on them they start shaking,
and when we have heard their prayer and woes.

And the trees will be green and the grass will be dry,

The water bright with foam as it breaks away from the shore;
The sun is down to rest itself for another day:
In the sky are only the stars which have no eyes that can see;
Only this old world's heart still beats within me: I am not dead,

Tanka, as you know, is one of Japan's most beloved and best-known poetic forms.

1) Tanka was created by a monk named Ki no Tomonori who lived during the Heian period (794–1185). According to legend, he composed this poem when his master asked him to describe the beauty of an autumn night sky: "I look at the moon, but I see no more than my own shadow;
